abstract = {This paper is based on the suggestion that integrated circuits containing many active components effectively reduce the cost per active component in a system. Some implications of this on the design of computing circuits are discussed in the light of the history of such circuits. Various techniques of making integrated circuits are mentioned in order to discuss the possible limitations of such techniques if applied to complex conventional digital circuits. Alternative, matrix-like, circuits are proposed which have properties suited to integration. A system consisting of an arithmetic unit and a control unit is briefly described as an illustration of possible ways of using matrix networks. Some broader implications of the methods suggested are discussed with respect to storage systems and future developments.}
