author = {Edward Tsang},
abstract = {Publisher Summary
This chapter explains the techniques that, by exploiting the specific features of a constraint satisfaction problem (CSP), attempt to either reduce the space searched or the complexity in computation. It discusses the fact that when the constraint graph of a CSP is a tree, one can solve this problem in O(na2), where n is the number of variables and a is the maximum domain size in the problem. The cycle-cutset method is a dynamic search method that can be applied to binary CSPs. When the cycle-cutset method is used together with chronological backtracking, a smaller space will normally be searched because of the use of directional arc-consistent after the cycle cutest is labeled. This may also be true when this method is used together with other search strategies. However, in some search strategies, for example, those that learn and those that look ahead and order the variables dynamically, the search sequence may be affected by the history of the search. Thus, it is not guaranteed that the cycle-cutset method will explore a smaller search space when coupled with these methods.}
}
