abstract = {Publisher Summary
There are several tools that are invaluable to the network engineer—in particular, the intrusion detection system (IDS). IDS is a constantly evolving technology, there are commercial versions of IDS, such as the older Cisco Netranger that is now the Host and Appliance Sensor, and open source IDS systems such as Snort. Along with the IDS come signature files and various graphical front ends and SQL databases for storing log files. An IDS sensor works in simple terms, much like an alarm system for a house. There are many obstacles to deploying an effective IDS solution. Problems range from report generation to where to place the sensor(s) or how to diminish the false positives that occur. A different approach to network security is not really security but in the near future will become much more important to anyone with a server on the Internet. To protect the demilitarized zone (DMZ) and to have the DMZ protect the network is no small undertaking. It involves examining the network, all the way from the big-picture enterprise view down to the desktop and if the servers are locked away in a secure room.}
}
