pages = {231-309},
year = {2004},
isbn = {978-1-931836-04-3},
doi = {https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-193183604-3/50011-4},
url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9781931836043500114},
author = {Andrew R. Baker and Brian Caswell and Mike Poor and Stephen Northcutt and Raven Alder and Jacob Babbin and Jay Beale and Adam Doxtater and James C. Foster and Toby Kohlenberg and Michael Rash},
abstract = {Publisher Summary
This chapter examines the role of preprocessors in relation to rules, how Snort's existing preprocessors can be used and tuned, and how a preprocessor can be build. Preprocessors are extremely useful and they make rules easier to write, lower false positive/negative counts, and give a rule-matching intrusion detection system (IDS) the capability to exceed its traditionally simple detection model while maintaining performance. The chapter examines each of the major purposes for which preprocessors are used, including reassembling packets, decoding protocols, and nonrule or anomaly-based detection. Snort has two preprocessor plug-ins assisting the rule-matching by combining data spread across multiple packets—stream4 and frag2. The two goals of stream4—TCP statefulness and session reassembly—are also discussed in the chapter. Stateless devices only look at one packet at a time—they have no memory of the previous packets. This means that their only way of gauging the status of a session is to look at the combination of flags. The frag2 is useful in detecting fragment-based denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. These attacks often send a series of well-designed fragments to take advantage of a host's particular IP stack vulnerabilities.}
}
